# XSIAM Ingestion Health Monitor

## Purpose

The XSIAM Ingestion Health Monitor is an hourly operational check intended to identify a sustained ingestion-health risk and notify the Monitoring and Integration Platform (MIP) team before it becomes a material visibility gap.

The monitor is designed to send warning, high and critical notifications to the **Data Health ingestion** Teams channel. Critical or re-escalated conditions can additionally use PagerDuty when that integration is approved and configured.

> **Current status: pending production deployment.** The monitor package has been prepared and its notification behaviour has been locally verified. It must not be treated as a live operational control until the outstanding approval, integration, deployment and test activities are complete.

## Monitoring schedule and thresholds

| Item | Design |
| --- | --- |
| Health check schedule | Every hour |
| Daily summary | 08:00 Europe/London |
| Data source | Cortex XSIAM read-only API |
| Warning condition | Ingestion is below the warning threshold for the configured sustained period |
| High condition | Ingestion is below the high threshold for the configured sustained period |
| Critical condition | Ingestion is below the critical threshold for the configured sustained period |
| Notification destinations | Teams for all new alerts; PagerDuty only for critical or re-escalated alerts when enabled |

Exact thresholds and sustained-period settings are deployment configuration. They must be reviewed with the owning operational team and recorded in the approved deployment configuration.

## Alert ownership and response

The intended operational owner is the Out-of-Hours (OOH) or Continuous Integration (CI) team. The agreed owner should:

1. acknowledge and triage new high and critical alerts;
2. identify whether the condition is a source, connector, platform or licensing issue;
3. follow the relevant XSIAM runbook and record evidence;
4. escalate material visibility gaps through the agreed incident route; and
5. hand over any unfinished action using the [out-of-hours response handover template](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/monitoring-and-integration-platform/blob/main/docs/out_of_hours_handover.md).

## Required production-readiness actions

The following actions remain open and are tracked in [Jira MON-5460](https://dsdmoj.atlassian.net/browse/MON-5460):

1. Obtain a scoped Power Platform approval for the HTTP connector in the MIP environment. The tenant-wide `MoJ-Blocked-HTTP` policy must not be changed for this monitor.
2. Configure the Teams workflow for the **Data Health ingestion** channel and store its workflow URL as `TeamsWorkflowUrl` in the approved Azure Key Vault.
3. Deploy the Function App and its infrastructure through the approved CI/CD route.
4. Run an end-to-end alert test, including notification delivery and acknowledgement ownership.
5. Confirm the OOH or CI support model, escalation contacts and optional PagerDuty configuration.
6. Complete the applicable change, security and service-acceptance checks before enabling operational alerts.

## Security and secret handling

The monitor uses a read-only XSIAM API credential. Credentials, API keys, workflow URLs and any secret values must be stored only in the approved secret-management service. They must not be committed to this repository, added to Jira comments, or posted to Teams.

## Architecture

```text
Cortex XSIAM read-only API
          |
          v
Hourly ingestion-health Function
          |
          +--> Azure Key Vault (configuration and secrets)
          |
          +--> Teams: Data Health ingestion (warning, high and critical)
          |
          +--> PagerDuty (critical/re-escalated only; optional)
          |
          v
OOH or CI triage, escalation and handover
```

